ugh a combination of artistic intention, technical execution, consistency, audience comprehension, and production reliability. Useful indicators for game audio include responsiveness, clarity, repetition tolerance, spatial accuracy, mix balance, memory use, and emotional contribution. No single metric can capture creative value, but clear criteria make feedback more specific and revisions more productive.\n\n## Creative intention meets production reality\n\nEntertainment production is a negotiation between ambition and constraints. Time, budget, access, performer availability, technology, rights, platform requirements, and audience expectations can all change what is possible. Constraints do not automatically reduce quality. Often they force a project to decide what matters most.\n\nThe strongest teams make those trade-offs explicit. They distinguish decisions that define the identity of the work from decisions that can change without damaging it. That distinction helps a production protect its central idea while still adapting to technical problems, schedule pressure, feedback, or distribution needs.\n\n## Collaboration and iteration\n\nCreative work becomes more reliable when feedback is attached to a clear objective. A note such as \"make it better\" is difficult to act on. A note about pacing, clarity, continuity, tone, audience understanding, technical quality, or a specific rights problem can be tested.\n\nIteration also changes with production stage. Early revisions are cheap and broad. Late revisions are usually narrower because more departments and assets depend on earlier choices. This is why experienced productions try to identify structural problems before expensive finishing work begins.\n\n## Evaluating the result\n\nFor game audio, useful evaluation criteria include responsiveness, clarity, repetition tolerance, spatial accuracy, mix balance, memory use, and emotional contribution. These criteria do not replace artistic judgement. They make judgement more accountable by asking what the work is trying to achieve and whether the production choices support that aim.\n\nAudience response can add another layer of evidence, but popularity and quality are not identical. A project can be technically accomplished and reach a small audience, or attract a large audience while still containing craft problems. Good evaluation separates commercial outcomes, audience outcomes, and creative outcomes instead of treating them as one score.\n\n## Rights, safety, and professional practice\n\nEntertainment work also carries obligations beyond the finished artefact. Risks can include hearing safety, performer rights, music licences, voice consent, and asset provenance. Strong production practice documents ownership, permissions, credits, approvals, and safety responsibilities early enough that they can shape decisions rather than become emergencies.\n\nAccessibility belongs in the same conversation. Captions, audio description, readable graphics, navigable digital interfaces, venue access, and other accommodations can expand who is able to experience the work and can improve clarity for wider audiences.\n\n## What to remember\n\nGame audio becomes easier to understand when craft, production systems, audience experience, rights, and safety are treated as connected. The most durable creative organisations learn from each project, document what worked, and improve the process as deliberately as they improve the final work.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel entertainment explainer. Exact rules, contracts, technical standards, and platform requirements can change, so current primary documentation should be checked when a real production decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/entertainment-fallback-3.png","featured_image_alt":"Game audio: How quality is evaluated","word_count":642,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["entertainment","evergreen","explainer","Game audio"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"AES","title":"AES","url":"https://www.aes.org/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"UNESCO","title":"UNESCO Culture","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/culture","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WIPO","title":"World Intellectual Property Organization","url":"https://www.wipo.int/copyright/en/","authority":"primary","position":2}]}]$blogdel$::jsonb) as p(
  external_id text, published_at timestamptz, slug text, title text,
  description text, body_markdown text, article_type text, language text,
  featured_image_url text, featured_image_alt text, word_count integer,
  reading_time_minutes integer, keywords text[], provider text, model text,
  image_source_type text, image_provider text, image_model text, refs jsonb
);

insert into public.source_items (
  source_id, category_id, external_id, prompt, context, refs, instructions, status, source_published_at
)
select
  s.id, c.id, p.external_id,
  'Canonical GPT-5.6 Sol Entertainment archive article: ' || p.title,
  jsonb_build_object('headline',p.title,'published_at',p.published_at),
  p.refs,
  jsonb_build_object(
    'article_type','explainer',
    'tone','clear',
    'freshness','evergreen',
    'required_author_provider','chatgpt',
    'required_author_model','gpt-5.6-sol',
    'benchmark_cohort','sol-historical-archive-v1',
    'forbid_blogdel_text_generation',true,
    'image_policy','unique-first-fallback-allowed'
  ),
  'processed'::public.source_item_status,
  p.published_at
from _blogdel_ent_payload p
join public.categories c on c.slug='entertainment'
join public.sources s on s.category_id=c.id and s.slug='culture-media-evergreen' and s.is_enabled
where not exists (select 1 from public.source_items si where si.external_id=p.external_id);

update public.source_items si
set status='processed',
    rejection_reason=null,
    source_published_at=p.published_at,
    context=jsonb_build_object('headline',p.title,'published_at',p.published_at),
    refs=p.refs,
    instructions=coalesce(si.instructions,'{}'::jsonb) || jsonb_build_object(
      'required_author_provider','chatgpt',
      'required_author_model','gpt-5.6-sol',
      'benchmark_cohort','sol-historical-archive-v1',
      'forbid_blogdel_text_generation',true,
      'image_policy','unique-first-fallback-allowed'
    )
from _blogdel_ent_payload p
where si.external_id=p.external_id;

update public.articles a
set slug=p.slug,title=p.title,description=p.description,body_markdown=p.body_markdown,
    article_type=p.article_type::public.article_type_enum,language=p.language,
    status='published'::public.article_status_enum,featured_image_url=p.featured_image_url,
    featured_image_alt=p.featured_image_alt,word_count=p.word_count,
    reading_time_minutes=p.reading_time_minutes,keywords=p.keywords,
    provider='chatgpt',model='gpt-5.6-sol',is_demo=false,event_at=p.published_at,
    published_at=p.published_at,archived_at=null,generation_job_id=null,
    image_source_type=p.image_source_type,image_provider=p.image_provider,
    image_model=p.image_model,updated_at=now()
from _blogdel_ent_payload p
join public.source_items si on si.external_id=p.external_id
where a.source_item_id=si.id;

insert into public.articles (
  source_item_id,category_id,author_id,generation_job_id,slug,title,description,
  body_markdown,article_type,language,status,featured_image_url,featured_image_alt,
  word_count,reading_time_minutes,keywords,provider,model,is_demo,event_at,
  published_at,image_source_type,image_provider,image_model
)
select
  si.id,si.category_id,au.id,null,p.slug,p.title,p.description,p.body_markdown,
  p.article_type::public.article_type_enum,p.language,'published'::public.article_status_enum,
  p.featured_image_url,p.featured_image_alt,p.word_count,p.reading_time_minutes,p.keywords,
  'chatgpt','gpt-5.6-sol',false,p.published_at,p.published_at,
  p.image_source_type,p.image_provider,p.image_model
from _blogdel_ent_payload p
join public.source_items si on si.external_id=p.external_id
join lateral (
  select id from public.authors where category_id=si.category_id and is_active
  order by last_used_at asc nulls first limit 1
) au on true
where not exists (select 1 from public.articles a where a.source_item_id=si.id);

delete from public.article_references ar
using public.articles a, public.source_items si, _blogdel_ent_payload p
where ar.article_id=a.id and a.source_item_id=si.id and si.external_id=p.external_id;

insert into public.article_references (
  article_id,source_item_id,provider,title,url,authority,position,retrieved_at
)
select a.id,si.id,ref->>'provider',ref->>'title',ref->>'url',
       nullif(ref->>'authority',''),
       coalesce((ref->>'position')::integer,ordinality::integer-1),now()
from _blogdel_ent_payload p
join public.source_items si on si.external_id=p.external_id
join public.articles a on a.source_item_id=si.id
cross join lateral jsonb_array_elements(p.refs) with ordinality as r(ref,ordinality);

commit;
